        "userMessage": "Conversation info (untrusted metadata): ```json { \"message_id\": \"23638\", \"reply_to_id\": \"23637\", \"sender_id\": \"8518484672\", \"sender\": \"Jonathan\", \"timestamp\": \"Wed 2026-04-15 05:13 PDT\", \"has_reply_context\": true } ``` Sender (untrusted metadata): ```json { \"label\": \"Jonathan (8518484672)\", \"id\": \"8518484672\", \"name\": \"Jonathan\" } ``` Replied message (untrusted, for context): ```json { \"sender_label\": \"guclaw\", \"body\": \"Short version, learned_route is worse mostly because it is more expensive for the same answer, not because it is obviously dumber.\\n\\nWhat I found:\\n\\n• On the latest per-trace rollup, learned_route is better on 7, worse on 16, tied on 0 against graph_prior_only (Source: artifacts/openclawbrain-proof-cron/aggregate/rolling-aggregate.md#L10).\\n• The comparator now checks, in order:\\n 1. success\\n 2. phrase misses\\n 3. compile misses\\n 4. quality\\n 5. warnings\\n 6. selected context chars\\n 7. estimated prompt tokens\\n(Source: openclawbrain/scripts/proof-cron.mjs#L631)\\n\\nThe important part:\\n\\n• For all 16 latest per-trace losses, learned_route lost on selectedContextChars, not on success, compile, or quality.\\n• In plain English, it usually got the same result as graph_prior_only, but pulled longer context to do it.\\n• It also used the same number of selected blocks, so this is not “more hops”, it is “heavier blocks”.\\n\\nThat is why the old diagnostic framing was misleading:\\n\\n• many of those traces are still diagnostic ties at the top-score layer\\n• but under the real product metric, ties are not good enough if one route burns more context for the same outcome\\n(Source: artifacts/openclawbrain-proof-cron/aggregate/rolling-aggregate.md#L18)\\n\\nConcrete pattern I saw:\\n\\n• learned_route often swaps in runtime-graph event/split nodes where graph_prior_only uses a more compact seed/pack/workspace node.\\n• Example shapes:\\n • tern-recorded-session-proof, learned route used 1077 chars vs 908\\n • trace-retrieval-routing-prior-doc, learned route used 1311 vs 1216\\n • real-trace-live-proof-story, learned route used 1897 vs 1768\\n\\nSo my blunt read is:\\n\\nWhy it is worse\\n\\n1. It is over-selecting verbose nodes\\n • same coverage, longer snippets\\n2. It is not compactness-aware enough\\n • the route is finding relevant context, but not the cheapest sufficient context\\n3. It may be preferring runtime graph nodes over cleaner seed/workspace nodes\\n • especially split / duplicate / event-heavy no…[truncated]\" } ``` I think I want a bigger, more realistic eval set",
